<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jebel Aruda</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jebel Aruda is an archaeological site on a hill above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habuba-kabira">Habuba Kabira</a>, excavated in the 1970s and early 1980s during the salvage campaigns for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabqa-dam">Tabqa Dam</a>. It contained a settlement with large tripartite temples and houses of southern Mesopotamian type, built during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a> expansion in the late fourth millennium BCE.</p></div>
